<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saida</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sidon, or Saida, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, about 40 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, inhabited since prehistoric times. One of the most important Phoenician cities, it was known for its glass and purple dye, and under Persian rule it eclipsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> as the leading city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> took it in 1110 and made it the seat of the Lordship of Sidon, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> it was the capital of a province. It is now the third-largest city in Lebanon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidon.jpg" alt="Sidon" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heretiq · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SidonNewCity.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sidon stands on a mainland promontory with two harbours. Its Phoenician name, Ṣīdūn, probably meant fishery or fishing town. As a Roman colony it was renamed Colonia Aurelia Pia Sidon, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> called it Sagittus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Saete or Sayette in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a>. Excavations show that the site has been inhabited since early prehistory: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tool</a> assemblage of the Acheulean was found at Sidon II, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> one at Sidon III. A settlement of the late Early Bronze Age I lies near the ruined Crusader castle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sidon may have been the oldest of the Phoenician cities, and a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trading network grew from it and other ports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> praised the skill of its craftsmen in glass and purple dye and of its women in embroidery. Tradition says a colonising party from Sidon founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, and the two cities later each claimed to be the mother city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. Around 1350 BCE it was part of the Egyptian Empire and ruled by Zimredda.</p><p>It was conquered in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Persian rule began in the 6th century BCE, and in exchange for its help in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> gave Sidon the territories of Dor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a> and the Plain of Sharon. Alexander the Great occupied it in 333 BCE.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> the city kept minting its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, and Herod built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> there. On 4 December 1110 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I of Jerusalem</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-the-crusader">Sigurd I of Norway</a> captured it, and it became the centre of the Lordship of Sidon, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>. Saladin took it in 1187, and German Crusaders restored Christian rule in 1197. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> destroyed it in 1249 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> again in 1260.</p><p>After Ottoman Turkish rule began in the early 16th century it became the capital of the Sidon Eyalet and regained much of its earlier commercial importance. In the 18th century the Hammud family of notables dominated the city and monopolised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> exports. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was part of the French Mandate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sidon is the capital of the South Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> are each about 40 kilometres away. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon-sea-castle">Sidon Sea Castle</a>, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> in the early 13th century, stands near the port. The Khan al-Franj is a 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> named for the French merchants it housed, though it was once wrongly credited to Emir Fakhreddine II. The Sidon Soap Museum traces the history of soap making in the region. Palestinian refugees who arrived after 1948 were settled in the camps of Ein el-Hilweh and Mieh Mieh.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sidon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Iron Age, Byblos lacked the fleets of Tyre and <b>Sidon</b> and generally submitted to the empires that dominated the Levant rather than resisting them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Close ties with the Phoenician cities, sealed by Ahab’s marriage to Jezebel, daughter of the king of <b>Sidon</b>, brought luxury goods and craftsmen, and the carved ivory panels found at Samaria show the court’s taste for Phoenician work.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…Fatimids and extending the kingdom along the coast with the help of Genoese, Pisan and Venetian fleets, taking Arsuf and Caesarea in 1101, Acre in 1104 and Beirut and <b>Sidon</b> in 1110, and pushing south-east of the Dead Sea, where he built the castle of Montréal in 1115.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Iron Age, as Egyptian power over Canaan declined, peoples and cities formed regional and national cults round a patron god: Chemosh of the Moabites, Milcom of the Ammonites, Qos of the Edomites, Yahweh of the Israelites, Melqart of Tyre and Eshmun of <b>Sidon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>The cities of the Lebanese coast, Tyre, <b>Sidon</b>, Byblos, Arwad and Beirut among them, came through comparatively unharmed and, freed from Egyptian and Hittite overlords, emerged as independent kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Sumerian cities were ruled by lords and kings who governed in the name of the city’s god; Phoenician Tyre and <b>Sidon</b> by kings with councils of elders; the Classic Maya cities by holy lords, k’uhul ajaw.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
